{{smaller block|to be religious, fain he would ''bear up with'' his neighbours in that.
{{right|''Milton'', Areopagitica, p. 39.}}}}

'''To bear with''', to endure; be indulgent to; forbear to resent, oppose, or punish.
{{smaller block|Reason would that I should ''bear with'' you.
{{right|Acts xviii. 14.}}
If the matter be meane, and meanly handled, I pray you ''beare'' both ''with'' me and it.
{{right|''Ascham'', The Scholemaster, p. 22.}}}}

'''To bring to bear.''' See ''bring.''

